WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:05.841
[Danny Hillis] It was getting to be the early
90s and people were still thinking about the

00:00:05.841 --> 00:00:07.716
future as being in the year 2000.

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:12.887
You know, I realized that the future had kind
of been shrinking by one year per year 

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:14.847
for my whole life.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:20.449
That's when I started designing this clock
that would last for 10,000 years.

00:00:24.439 --> 00:00:27.859
[Stewart Brand] I heard from Danny Hillis
about his idea to build a Stonehenge-scale

00:00:27.942 --> 00:00:32.779
mechanical clock that would tick once a century,
bong every thousand years, and,

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:36.032
after much longer than that, the cuckoo comes out.

00:00:42.496 --> 00:00:48.950
[Danny] Steward realized the 10 000 year clock
would change how people thought about time

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:52.296
long before I did, even when I was building it.

00:00:52.338 --> 00:01:19.028
[music]

00:01:19.028 --> 00:02:15.495
[music and ambient noise]

00:02:16.537 --> 00:02:30.007
[Stewart] The Clock is an artwork that is
already an expression of thinking long term.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:40.280
[Brian Eno] The Clock is a provocation, in
a sense.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:44.437
Is it possible that we could be around for
10 000 years?

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:45.690
What if there's a war?

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:51.150
Or, what if asteroid hits the Earth and there's
a nuclear winter?

00:02:51.151 --> 00:02:59.408
Even saying "what if" is starting to do what
the clock is intended to do.

00:03:00.409 --> 00:03:24.330
[Bells Peal]

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:38.260
[Music]

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:44.823
Once you are holding these longer time frames
in mind that starts to raise the question of

00:03:44.865 --> 00:03:51.150
What do you do on Monday?

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:55.879
Does your behavior start to reflect this larger
frame?

00:03:55.879 --> 00:03:56.430
[Music]

